{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Is the composition invariant guarded? It is now — and the first version of the guard had a hole\n\nStating this as a decision rather than leaving it implicit: **the invariant is BUILD-ENFORCED,\nnot a convention.** `test_every_wall_clock_bound_goes_through_clamped` is a source scan over\n`observability.sh` that fails if `timeout` or `bounded` appears in command position anywhere\noutside the two lines that form the door (`bounded`'s own definition, and `clamped`'s single\ndelegation to it).\n\nA source scan rather than a runtime check, deliberately: the grant tests observe only the calls\na fixture actually reaches, so a bare `timeout` on a branch behind a host condition the stubs\ndo not produce would pass them. The scan sees every call site whether or not any test executes\nit.\n\n### The guard did not initially hold, and the check is why I know\n\nAsked whether it was guarded, I checked rather than asserted, and the honest first answer was\n**partly**. The original scan matched only `timeout \"$VAR\"` and `bounded <digits>`. So I\nreintroduced the exact shape named as the risk — a bare `timeout 15 docker ps`, which is the\nform two of the original nine ceilings actually had — and:\n\n- the **source scan passed**. It did not see it.\n- the **runtime grant test failed**, catching it by accident of coverage.\n\nCaught by the weaker of the two mechanisms, for a reason that would not generalise. Had that\ncall sat on a branch the fixture does not reach, both would have passed and the composition bug\nwould have been back with no signal. The scan now matches either helper in command position\nwith any argument, and I re-ran the mutations rather than trusting the regex:\n\n| reintroduced shape | before | after |\n|---|---|---|\n| `timeout 15 docker ps` (bare literal) | **MISSED** | caught |\n| `timeout \"$VAR_TMP_DU_TIMEOUT\" du -sx` | caught | caught |\n| `bounded 15 git ls-remote` | caught | caught |\n| `bounded \"$JOURNAL_DU_TIMEOUT\" du -sx` | caught | caught |\n| a brand-new `timeout 30 …` at line start | **MISSED** | caught |\n| a brand-new `timeout 30 …` after a pipe | **MISSED** | caught |\n\nThree of six shapes were invisible to the first version. The scan over-matches and then\nsubtracts the two sanctioned lines by exact text, so a new spelling of the door fails loudly\ninstead of quietly widening it.\n\nThe rationale is now also stated at the definition site in `observability.sh`, so the next\nperson adding a bounded call reads it before the test tells them.\n\n## The accepted degradation is filed\n\n`docker_storage_bytes` / `docker_unaccounted_bytes` going intermittently silent under load is a\n**chosen trade**, and an undocumented regression the moment it lives only in a handoff message.\nFiled as **`utopic-frenzied-albino`** (`5993-4cf7-0de9-4f72`, p2), linked `discovered_from` this\nticket, carrying the 95 s and 125 s negative results, why 60 s is still correct given the\nbimodal distribution, what is lost (`docker_unaccounted_bytes` is the incident metric), four\noptions with none pre-selected, and the alarm question a deliberate non-measurement raises\nagainst `treat_missing_data = \"breaching\"`.\n\nThe distinguishing signal is recorded there and in the script: a gap with `docker_du_seconds`\n≈ 60 and `probe_ok` = 1 is the trade working as designed; a gap with `probe_ok` absent or\n`probe_truncated` = 1 belongs to this ticket's revert conditions.\n"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788643493185828247,"uuid":"175013fc-1a7b-4cac-9f0e-559a644bebf1"}